00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:10.100
Now, what this is all about is that
whenever we have a reversible reaction,

00:00:10.129 --> 00:00:14.379
meaning we have a forward and the
reverse, when they are at equilibrium,

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.989
it is possible to disturb that
equilibrium, to apply a stress to it,

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.560
meaning that the reaction can shift.

00:00:21.869 --> 00:00:25.020
It can either get pushed towards
the forward or towards the reverse.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:29.720
And it's all based on different
conditions we apply to the reaction.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:34.050
So the actual official definition of
this is that whenever you place an

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:39.670
external stressor on a reaction at
equilibrium, it has to respond to it.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:44.210
So like one example to this is, you
know, if you, if you're in your classroom

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.730
and your teacher tells you, Hey, you
have a test next, next class, then all

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.150
of a sudden your heart starts racing.

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.339
That's like, Oh no, you know,
I got to start studying.

00:00:52.339 --> 00:00:53.120
I got to prepare for this.

00:00:53.535 --> 00:00:56.775
There's all the, all this different things
that start racing through your brain.

00:00:57.035 --> 00:00:58.095
That's a stress, right?

00:00:58.095 --> 00:01:00.985
And your body's going to respond
by telling you to study and

00:01:01.195 --> 00:01:03.115
prepare and do and respond to that.

00:01:03.764 --> 00:01:08.295
So whenever the system responds within
a chemical reaction, the rate of the

00:01:08.295 --> 00:01:14.145
forward or the reverse reaction changes
either to increase or decrease in

00:01:14.145 --> 00:01:16.155
order to try to relieve that stress.

00:01:16.155 --> 00:01:19.744
So we'll be talking about it
shifting left, right, in terms of.

