Well, that means you're going to be
raising this side up as a result,

00:13:59.064 --> 00:14:00.555
so it's going to get a lot hotter.

00:14:01.165 --> 00:14:05.045
So when that's getting hotter, what
is it going to be essentially doing?

00:14:05.385 --> 00:14:09.725
Well, we're going to be shifting
that reaction to respond to that.

00:14:10.115 --> 00:14:14.245
It's going to shift the reaction in
the other direction, just like how

00:14:14.245 --> 00:14:17.675
we did earlier with concentration.

00:14:17.905 --> 00:14:20.284
We're going to raise that side up.

00:14:20.485 --> 00:14:24.184
So that way it's going to shift
in the, uh, and essentially

00:14:24.184 --> 00:14:25.654
in the other direction, right?

00:14:25.655 --> 00:14:29.375
So when we shift that, so
if that increases, we are

00:14:29.375 --> 00:14:31.175
going to shift away from it.

00:14:32.105 --> 00:14:33.565
So we will do that.

00:14:33.565 --> 00:14:34.825
So add away.

00:14:35.045 --> 00:14:35.925
So we add heat.

00:14:35.945 --> 00:14:37.805
We're going to shift away from heat.

00:14:38.575 --> 00:14:38.885
All right.

00:14:38.885 --> 00:14:40.005
So that's what will happen there.

00:14:40.825 --> 00:14:43.195
Now, what if I wanted to
decrease that temperature?

00:14:43.504 --> 00:14:45.735
Well, it's going to be
generally the same idea, right?

00:14:45.745 --> 00:14:52.394
If I decrease that temperature, um, and
we want the system to get a lot colder

00:14:52.424 --> 00:14:57.655
as a result, well, we can essentially say
that, well, the reaction is super cold.

00:14:57.655 --> 00:14:59.855
So it's going to want to respond to that.

00:15:00.274 --> 00:15:02.644
by it wanting to go in
the opposite direction.

00:15:03.225 --> 00:15:08.215
So if we go really cold with our
heat, it's going to respond and want

00:15:08.225 --> 00:15:11.755
to go forward to generate more heat.

00:15:12.075 --> 00:15:16.055
It's going to, once we take away a
substance, it's going to want to go

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:18.900
towards that substance is the whole idea.

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:20.839
So that's how we can look at temperature.

00:15:21.459 --> 00:15:22.530
Let's look at another though.

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:25.699
Sometimes when it comes to reactions,
you might not be given an actual

00:15:25.699 --> 00:15:29.930
numerical value and I'll just say, Hey,
there's heat involved in this problem.

00:15:30.459 --> 00:15:33.689
So with this one, it says, what will
happen if you increase the temperature?

00:15:33.979 --> 00:15:36.400
So we'd still treat it as XR endothermic.

00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:39.459
So that's your responsibility
to kind of remember that trend.

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:44.370
So if it's on the left hand side, that
meant that it's absorbing heat, that's

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:46.880
endothermic is how we can look at that.

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:53.750
So if I want to increase that heat,
well, we are going to be adding heat.

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:54.710
So what's it going to do?

00:15:54.710 --> 00:15:56.579
It's going to shift away from it.

00:15:56.829 --> 00:16:00.760
So that means it's going to
shift to the right hand side to

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:02.980
generate more product in this case.

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:08.555
Now if we had the opposite here Where
let's say if I wanted to decrease

00:16:08.555 --> 00:16:12.104
that, well, how is the system
going to respond by decreasing?

00:16:12.555 --> 00:16:14.645
So I'm going to make
this really, really cold.

00:16:14.954 --> 00:16:17.915
Well, that means that the system's
going to want to generate to

00:16:17.915 --> 00:16:20.744
try to leave that equilibrium.

00:16:21.194 --> 00:16:25.144
So it's going to shift towards
the heat direction, right?

00:16:25.414 --> 00:16:27.525
If you take it away, it's going to shift.

00:16:27.844 --> 00:16:29.485
towards that substance.

00:16:29.865 --> 00:16:31.995
Let's take a look at our next factor.

00:16:32.095 --> 00:16:33.095
Pressure change.

00:16:33.125 --> 00:16:37.565
When it comes to pressure, these different
changes all have to do with gases.

00:16:37.974 --> 00:16:41.254
So when we talk about pressure and
volume, we're talking about different

00:16:41.254 --> 00:16:45.295
gas molecules because essentially
the size of their container does

00:16:45.324 --> 00:16:50.045
affect how they react because gas
particles are very, very far apart.

00:16:50.545 --> 00:16:53.765
So if a container is really
small, then the likelihood of them

00:16:53.765 --> 00:16:55.595
colliding with each other increases.

00:16:56.004 --> 00:16:59.254
So it's all about how big or how
small that container is, which will

00:16:59.294 --> 00:17:00.964
increase or decrease the pressure.

00:17:01.475 --> 00:17:04.365
So if I have a container
with a bunch of gases in it.

00:17:04.865 --> 00:17:08.375
The reaction is going to
shift to reduce pressure.

00:17:08.735 --> 00:17:10.325
Now, how can it do that?

00:17:10.355 --> 00:17:13.325
Well, it's all about the
number of moles of gas.

00:17:13.745 --> 00:17:19.885
So if I am to look at this, uh, particular
reaction, you can count the coefficients.

00:17:20.235 --> 00:17:21.975
So if you see the reactants, right?

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:28.085
So two A plus b, I have two A
molecules and I have one B molecule.

00:17:28.355 --> 00:17:34.055
So that means you have a total of
three reactants on the left hand side.

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:38.030
And then if you look at the right
hand side, I have on my products,

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:41.350
I have one C and then one D.

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:44.760
So that means you have
a total of two products.

00:17:45.039 --> 00:17:49.729
So that means depending on how the
system responds, we'll essentially

00:17:49.739 --> 00:17:51.609
try to alleviate that pressure.

00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:55.340
So let's say if I increase the
pressure a lot, what it's going

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:58.800
to do, it's going to want to have
less moles of gas present to make.

00:17:59.160 --> 00:18:03.770
It will, the pressure will go down
if there's a very, very low pressure.

00:18:03.770 --> 00:18:07.920
Well, then it's going to try to fill that
void by going in the opposite direction.

00:18:08.469 --> 00:18:11.129
And it's also all about what are
the probability of collisions

00:18:11.129 --> 00:18:14.970
occurring to if you have, let's
say, a really small container.

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:16.860
That means a very, very high pressure.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:21.170
Well, the likelihood likelihood Of
the three moles of reactants running

00:18:21.170 --> 00:18:23.040
into each other increases by a lot.

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:26.140
So that's another reason why
it shifts in one direction.

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:27.660
Is what's the size of the container?

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:31.500
Who are you likely to run
into for a reaction to happen?

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:32.060
Right?

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:35.449
You have to, A has to run into
B for that reaction to happen.

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:38.440
C has to run into D for
that reaction to happen.

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:42.540
It's all about what is the probability
of the collisions occurring.

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:47.120
So if we were to look at this
one, what would happen if the

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:48.989
pressure were to decrease?

00:18:49.239 --> 00:18:53.449
Well, if you remember from before
where we have three moles of gas on

00:18:53.449 --> 00:18:55.999
the left, two moles on the right.

00:18:56.419 --> 00:18:59.510
So it's going to try to
shift to compensate for this.

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:04.250
So if the pressure is really, really low,
it's going to want to bring it back up.

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:07.040
So it's going to want to
create more moles of gas.

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:09.230
So when that happens.

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:13.490
We're going to be shifting the
reaction to the left in order

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:15.970
to compensate for that, right?

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:19.669
It's going to want to create
more gas molecules, uh, in,

00:19:19.689 --> 00:19:20.919
in, as a result of this.

00:19:21.355 --> 00:19:25.205
Another reason to is being at
this container, right there.

00:19:25.215 --> 00:19:28.885
It's going to be really big when it
has a low pressure, the likelihood

00:19:28.895 --> 00:19:33.685
of the that number of moles running
into each other decreases as a result.

00:19:33.695 --> 00:19:34.695
It actually becomes harder.

00:19:34.994 --> 00:19:38.445
It's more likely that 2 products
will find each other, hit each other.

00:19:38.935 --> 00:19:42.055
then it would be for three things to
hit each other all at the same time.

00:19:42.095 --> 00:19:43.125
It's a little rarer.

00:19:43.525 --> 00:19:47.435
So it's all about trying to figure out
how can you try to understand, see it.

00:19:48.054 --> 00:19:51.695
But I think the easiest way is a, the
less moles going to shift towards more.

00:19:51.695 --> 00:19:53.434
I think it's easy way to look at it.

00:19:54.275 --> 00:19:54.554
All right.

00:19:54.614 --> 00:19:57.975
Now what happens if the moles
were equal on both sides?

00:19:57.975 --> 00:19:59.245
So I rewrote the equation.

00:19:59.245 --> 00:20:00.045
So we have a new one.

00:20:00.305 --> 00:20:03.725
So it's just a plus B is C, C plus D.

00:20:04.375 --> 00:20:05.125
What would happen?

00:20:05.155 --> 00:20:09.474
Well, if both are the same and let's
say increase or decrease pressure.

00:20:09.895 --> 00:20:14.285
then it's not going to favor
one direction or the other.

00:20:14.755 --> 00:20:18.645
So even if you, uh, increase
or decrease the pressure, there

00:20:18.655 --> 00:20:19.934
won't be any change to it.

00:20:19.934 --> 00:20:20.885
It's not going to matter.

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:24.320
It's going to still be at equilibrium,
even if you're making them really close

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:29.489
together or really far apart, because
there's no favoritism on one side or

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:31.790
the other, there will be no change.

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:34.990
Um, so, and that's going
to be kind of one thing.

00:20:35.489 --> 00:20:38.659
Now, what if I added
another gas to the mixture?

00:20:38.659 --> 00:20:42.859
Let's say it was something that's inert,
like something that would increase

00:20:43.050 --> 00:20:45.705
the pressure Um, to the mixture.

00:20:45.705 --> 00:20:47.985
Would that matter in that case?

00:20:48.365 --> 00:20:51.945
Now, what being that is
not a part of the reaction.

00:20:52.334 --> 00:20:58.165
Um, if it's actually also not going to
have a change that's going to occur in the

00:20:58.165 --> 00:20:59.575
reaction, because it's not a part of it.

00:20:59.965 --> 00:21:03.345
So you, it's essentially affecting
both sides equally equally.

00:21:03.875 --> 00:21:09.254
So if we were to add something to
as a, almost like a, as a buffer in

00:21:09.254 --> 00:21:12.634
this case, where it's just this big
gas, it's all surrounding everything.

00:21:12.915 --> 00:21:14.295
Once again, it's going to be no change.

00:21:14.295 --> 00:21:17.564
It has to be a common gas or a
common ion to have an effect.

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:21.970
Before we actually look at trying to
understand volume, believe it or not,

00:21:21.970 --> 00:21:25.620
volume and pressure are actually very
intimately related to each other.

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:31.599
So if you remember as pressure increases,
volume decreases and vice versa, they

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:36.320
are an inverse relationship with one
another due to Boyle's law from gas laws.

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:41.050
So one thing about these containers that
if I, let's say, have a container of

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:43.439
gas and it's inside of a piston, Right.

00:21:43.470 --> 00:21:48.429
And it, what this thing is describing is
the reaction above where I have hydrogen

00:21:48.439 --> 00:21:50.550
combining with nitrogen to make ammonia.

00:21:51.280 --> 00:21:56.670
Now, if this, if this container is at a
full size and I want to make it smaller,

00:21:56.700 --> 00:22:01.395
where I push all the molecules together,
Notice how close everything is together.

00:22:01.395 --> 00:22:05.465
So the likelihood of things
reacting can increase immensely.

00:22:05.955 --> 00:22:11.024
So if I increase that, well, what
direction would the reaction likely shift?

00:22:11.564 --> 00:22:15.004
So if we kind of look at the
total number of moles here, right?

00:22:15.054 --> 00:22:16.984
I have four moles of gas.

00:22:17.504 --> 00:22:22.354
on the left hand side, and then they
have two moles of gas on the right.

00:22:22.764 --> 00:22:26.064
So meaning that if our pressure
goes up, it's going to want

00:22:26.084 --> 00:22:28.235
to relieve that pressure.

00:22:28.555 --> 00:22:30.955
So which direction is it
going to push the reaction?

00:22:31.035 --> 00:22:34.675
Well, it's going to push it
forward to make less molecules.

00:22:35.264 --> 00:22:39.124
And the way that we can see that if we
go from container B to container C, which

00:22:39.165 --> 00:22:43.164
is right here, right next to me, notice
how essentially the molecules are a

00:22:43.205 --> 00:22:45.695
little bit farther apart compared to B.

00:22:46.030 --> 00:22:50.890
And that's because we have less molecules
because we made more ammonia as a result.

00:22:51.380 --> 00:22:56.050
We shifted from going from just nitrogens
and hydrogens, like we see over here and

00:22:56.050 --> 00:22:59.390
they're all really close together, they're
going to be bouncing all over the walls.

00:22:59.769 --> 00:23:04.780
And it's going to create more reactions
to help relieve that pressure to mean

00:23:04.879 --> 00:23:07.039
get back to equilibrium as a result.

00:23:07.759 --> 00:23:09.550
So that's one thing that we can see.

00:23:09.550 --> 00:23:13.010
The goal is to relieve stress
by essentially creating

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:15.149
less molecules in the space.

00:23:15.149 --> 00:23:15.189
Thanks.

00:23:15.679 --> 00:23:19.729
So if we increase pressure, it's
going to shift towards less moles.

00:23:20.169 --> 00:23:24.460
If we decrease pressure, it's going to
want to go towards more moles as a result.

00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:28.519
Um, so now let's go look at volume, which
is going to be very similar to this.

00:23:28.529 --> 00:23:32.249
So the good news is that when
you change volume of a guess in a

00:23:32.249 --> 00:23:35.599
system, it's actually going to have
the exact same effect as pressure.

00:23:36.099 --> 00:23:40.039
Um, but you have to try to remember
that they are an inverse relationship.

00:23:40.399 --> 00:23:45.230
So you do got to keep in mind that,
uh, as like pressure increases,

00:23:45.240 --> 00:23:46.550
volume is going to decrease.

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:51.389
So you have to kind of remember those
little relationships that are here, right?

00:23:51.409 --> 00:23:53.330
As one goes up, the other
one's going to go down.

00:23:53.850 --> 00:23:57.029
So once again, though, the change
is determined by the number of

00:23:57.029 --> 00:23:59.479
moles, um, when we have this.

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:02.630
And it's important to also realize
that, well, what would happen if you

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:04.180
have the same moles on each side?

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:07.090
Well, it's going to once again
be no change like before.

00:24:07.610 --> 00:24:10.770
Um, and kind of going up to the
problem we had earlier, right?

00:24:11.249 --> 00:24:16.619
That if you can see here, well, when
pressure increase, volume also decrease.

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:20.989
You can see how much smaller
the space is within this piston.

00:24:21.485 --> 00:24:23.125
And it forced it forward.

00:24:23.495 --> 00:24:26.955
So it's the same relationship that's
here, but you got to keep in mind

00:24:26.955 --> 00:24:30.304
that when volume decreases, it's
going to shift it towards more moles.

00:24:30.784 --> 00:24:37.175
And when volume increases, it will shift
it towards, um, less moles in our, I

00:24:37.204 --> 00:24:39.745
think I might've said that backwards,
but essentially when you have the volume

00:24:39.745 --> 00:24:43.004
decrease, it's going to shift it to
less moles and the volume increase,

00:24:43.004 --> 00:24:44.314
it's going to go towards more moles.

00:24:44.455 --> 00:24:45.685
So same idea as pressure.

00:24:45.834 --> 00:24:46.144
All right.

00:24:46.725 --> 00:24:48.774
So that's one way to kind of
keep, keep that in mind when

00:24:48.774 --> 00:24:51.845
you're looking at it, it's going
to be the same idea as pressure.

00:24:51.845 --> 00:24:51.864
Okay.

00:24:52.544 --> 00:24:52.864
All right.

00:24:53.024 --> 00:24:54.465
So let's take a look at our last one.

00:24:54.635 --> 00:24:58.415
Catalysts and enzymes are one of
the most fundamental principles

00:24:58.415 --> 00:25:02.245
around biology because essentially
what keeps us metabolically

00:25:02.245 --> 00:25:04.074
functioning allows us to exist.

00:25:04.324 --> 00:25:09.114
So equilibrium is a big part of how our
body actually functions and works because

00:25:09.114 --> 00:25:13.145
it describes exactly how the chemical
reactions within those systems work.

00:25:13.544 --> 00:25:19.485
So if I have a catalyst, what it does,
it speeds up the rate of the reaction.

00:25:19.855 --> 00:25:22.585
by increasing the amount of collisions.

00:25:22.955 --> 00:25:25.385
So it's going to cause
things to occur faster.

00:25:25.905 --> 00:25:30.465
Now, when we have, let's say an enzyme,
the whole idea is when we have a reaction,

00:25:30.915 --> 00:25:37.815
essentially, there is an activation energy
barrier for the reaction to go over in

00:25:37.815 --> 00:25:40.155
order for the reaction to happen, right?

00:25:40.185 --> 00:25:44.395
On the left hand side, we have
our reactants and on the right

00:25:44.405 --> 00:25:46.295
hand side, we have our products.

00:25:46.675 --> 00:25:50.225
So that big hump or that
big hill is your barrier.

00:25:50.595 --> 00:25:55.450
So what essentially the enzyme is
trying to do is Hey, can we go around

00:25:55.460 --> 00:25:59.700
the mountain or find an alternate
path in order to make that a reality?

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:04.540
So when we add an enzyme, usually it's
like going around the side of the mountain

00:26:04.900 --> 00:26:07.989
in order to make this easier to occur.

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:10.910
It's less energy that is required.

00:26:11.449 --> 00:26:14.630
Now the thing about this though, is
you kind of noted, you got to think

00:26:14.630 --> 00:26:18.450
that, oh, it's not only about going
over the hill from one direction.

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:20.815
And yeah, There's people that
want to come from the harder

00:26:20.815 --> 00:26:22.435
side to the strenuous side.

00:26:22.755 --> 00:26:26.295
So both sides are
technically easier to cross.

00:26:26.775 --> 00:26:30.494
So that means both the forward and
the reverse reaction are easier.

00:26:31.094 --> 00:26:35.484
So as a result, that means that it
does not have an effect on equilibrium.

00:26:35.535 --> 00:26:39.574
Overall, the whole reaction is just
faster regardless of the direction.

00:26:39.944 --> 00:26:43.165
So which is kind of nice is that you don't
have to try to predict left or right.

00:26:43.385 --> 00:26:44.915
It's not going to have an effect.

00:26:46.545 --> 00:26:49.185
There are a couple important
things to mention about catalysts.

00:26:49.195 --> 00:26:51.825
So the first thing is they
are not reactants, they

00:26:51.825 --> 00:26:53.145
just speed up the reaction.

00:26:53.570 --> 00:26:58.899
And generally they're not consumed or
destroyed in the reaction when it occurs

00:26:58.899 --> 00:27:03.119
with the substrate they are bonding
to or the reactant they're bonding to.

00:27:03.639 --> 00:27:07.960
Um, they are just essentially, uh,
assisting the reaction to happen.

00:27:08.189 --> 00:27:12.659
They're kind of being like the crane arms
and the tools to help make these things

00:27:12.679 --> 00:27:15.040
actually get there a little bit faster.

00:27:15.400 --> 00:27:18.470
So the whole idea is that they
provide an alternate pathway.

00:27:18.830 --> 00:27:21.890
So the way that I like to look at it is
like, if you kind of go, let's say down

00:27:21.890 --> 00:27:25.565
a trail, And there's a sign there that
tells you all these different routes, and

00:27:25.565 --> 00:27:30.045
many of them might lead to the same exact
spot, but there's different ways for you

00:27:30.045 --> 00:27:33.754
to get there, different scenic routes
for you to take for you to get there.

00:27:34.090 --> 00:27:37.250
So, one way that I like to always look
at it is, let's say if I were to look,

00:27:37.300 --> 00:27:42.800
go on two different trails, well, when
you are hiking, well, how could you,

00:27:42.970 --> 00:27:44.859
how could you get over the mountain top?

00:27:45.139 --> 00:27:46.299
There are different routes.

00:27:46.299 --> 00:27:50.969
Like, you could essentially go up this
and maybe you could go all the way up

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:53.380
to the very, very top of the mountain.

00:27:53.685 --> 00:27:57.735
And then go over the backside
to get to the other part.

00:27:58.114 --> 00:27:58.985
That's one way to do it.

00:27:58.985 --> 00:27:59.195
Right?

00:27:59.215 --> 00:28:00.784
I'm sure it'd be a really beautiful hike.

00:28:00.894 --> 00:28:04.445
It's going to be really hard, but
there's one way to go around it.

00:28:04.945 --> 00:28:08.014
The other way is, let's say if you're
not interested in going, you don't

00:28:08.014 --> 00:28:09.334
want to go up over the mountain.

00:28:09.334 --> 00:28:11.105
You just want to get to the other side.

00:28:11.284 --> 00:28:12.175
And that's the goal.

00:28:12.495 --> 00:28:17.655
Well, maybe you could just go up the
pass, which is a little bit easier, or

00:28:17.705 --> 00:28:22.205
maybe you could even go all the way around
it and go through the valley as well.

00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:23.639
There are different routes.

00:28:23.639 --> 00:28:25.220
That's that's what enzymes do.

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:30.729
They offer different possibilities
to get around that peak, right?

00:28:30.739 --> 00:28:34.120
Do you want to go over the mountain
or do you just want to just enjoy

00:28:34.120 --> 00:28:37.409
a hike and go around and have
a little bit of an easier time?

00:28:38.030 --> 00:28:41.549
It's about trying to lower
that activation energy barrier.

00:28:41.885 --> 00:28:46.385
Whereas this guy all the way up here,
that's your activation energy barrier.

00:28:46.435 --> 00:28:48.945
That's a really huge
peak to try to conquer.

00:28:49.405 --> 00:28:51.195
So it provides an alternate path.

00:28:51.475 --> 00:28:56.135
So that way the system will reach
equilibrium faster is the entire idea.

00:28:56.655 --> 00:28:59.425
So that's the last part of
Le Chatelier's principle.

00:28:59.784 --> 00:29:02.774
So as you could see, this was a
lot of different concepts, that

00:29:02.794 --> 00:29:04.054
different factors that we could see.

00:29:04.054 --> 00:29:05.514
So let's go back to them real quick.

00:29:05.705 --> 00:29:08.425
As you can see, there was four
major factors that actually

00:29:08.435 --> 00:29:09.965
had an effect on equilibrium.

00:29:10.385 --> 00:29:12.045
catalyst and enzymes didn't.

00:29:12.045 --> 00:29:15.685
So it really comes down to knowing
how these four play a role.

00:29:15.985 --> 00:29:19.445
How essentially if I poke that
reaction with a stick, what

00:29:19.445 --> 00:29:20.685
is exactly going to happen?

00:29:21.195 --> 00:29:24.345
So with concentration and temperature,
they are very similar, right?

00:29:24.355 --> 00:29:28.315
If you, if you add, uh, add
something to the reaction, it's

00:29:28.315 --> 00:29:29.755
going to shift away from it.

00:29:30.155 --> 00:29:34.845
And if you take it away, it's going
to shift towards that substance.

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:38.520
With pressure and volume, they
have to do with the number of, the

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:42.140
coefficients, right, so the number
of moles that are in a reaction.

00:29:42.670 --> 00:29:47.220
So if you want to increase pressure
or decrease the volume, it's

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:49.469
going to shift towards less moles.

00:29:50.310 --> 00:29:54.309
If you, uh, decrease the pressure,
increase the volume, it's going

00:29:54.310 --> 00:29:56.042
to shift towards more moles.

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:59.370
So it's all about which
relationship do you have?

00:29:59.630 --> 00:30:00.100
All right.

00:30:00.490 --> 00:30:04.530
So, and then, and then with enzymes, it
did not have an effect on equilibrium.

